## 2. Expense Split Types

The DB schema supports all three split types from day one via the `split_type` column — **no migration needed when new split types ship**. Implementation is phased so the MVP ships sooner.

| `split_type` | Version | Behaviour | UI |
|---|---|---|---|
| `equal` | ✅ **MVP** | Amount ÷ N participants | Checkboxes — everyone selected gets an equal share |
| `exact` | 🔜 **v1.1** | Each participant gets a manually entered amount | Number input per person — must sum to total |
| `percentage` | 🔜 **v1.2** | Each participant gets a % of total | % input per person — must sum to 100% |

### MVP (equal split only)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **paid_by is always a participant.** Explicit design decision: the person who paid is always included in the split. If Swaroop pays ₹600 for groceries and selects 3 others, there are 4 participants total (₹150 each). Net balance: Swaroop paid ₹600, owes ₹150 → up ₹450. This is the Splitwise model. The UI must pre-check the payer's checkbox and disable it (non-removable).

- Backend: use **floor + remainder distribution** (not naive `round(amount/n, 2)` which silently drops paise)
- Frontend: checkboxes only — payer's checkbox is pre-checked and disabled
- Validation: must have ≥ 1 participant

**calculateShares algorithm (equal split):**
```js
function calculateEqualShares(amount, participantIds) {
  // Work in paise (integer) to avoid floating-point drift
  const totalPaise   = Math.round(amount * 100);
  const n            = participantIds.length;
  const floorPaise   = Math.floor(totalPaise / n);   // base share
  const remainder    = totalPaise % n;                // leftover paise to distribute

  // Sort by member created_at (oldest member first) so the extra paisa
  // lands predictably on whoever joined the group first — not a random UUID
  // NOTE: participantIds must be enriched with created_at before calling this
  const sorted = [...participants].sort(
    (a, b) => new Date(a.created_at) - new Date(b.created_at)
  );
  const sortedIds = sorted.map(p => p.member_id);

  return sortedIds.map((memberId, i) => ({
    member_id:    memberId,
    share_amount: ((floorPaise + (i < remainder ? 1 : 0)) / 100).toFixed(2),
  }));
  // Invariant: SUM(share_amount) === amount  ✅
}
```
> [!NOTE]
> This guarantees `SUM(shares) === amount` regardless of number of participants. Example: ₹100 ÷ 3 → [33.34, 33.33, 33.33] not [33.33, 33.33, 33.33].
> Sort order: oldest member (`created_at ASC`) gets the extra paisa first — deterministic and intentional rather than UUID-alphabetical.

### v1.1 (exact split)
- Backend: validate `SUM(share_amount) == expense.amount` (with float tolerance ±0.01)
- Frontend: number input appears per selected member
- Error code: `INVALID_SPLIT_AMOUNTS`

### v1.2 (percentage split)
- Backend: validate `SUM(share_percent) == 100`, then compute `share_amount = (percent / 100) * amount`
- Frontend: % input per member with live running total indicator
- Error code: `INVALID_SPLIT_PERCENTAGES`

