### Service Layer Contract


Controllers **only** call services. Services own:
- All Supabase queries (via `supabase.js` — service_role client, server-side only)
- Business logic (split calculation, balance computation)
- Sequential write coordination (no Supabase RPC)
- **Postgres error mapping** — catch raw DB errors and convert to typed `AppError`
- **Active member guard** — verify all referenced member IDs are alive before any DB write

**Active member guard (called at top of createExpense and createSettlement):**
```js
async function assertMembersActive(memberIds) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from('members')
    .select('id, deleted_at, is_active')
    .in('id', memberIds);

  if (error) throw new AppError('DB_QUERY_FAILED', 500, error.message);

  for (const m of data) {
    if (m.deleted_at || !m.is_active)
      throw new AppError('MEMBER_DELETED', 409,
        `Member ${m.id} has been removed from the group.`);
  }
  // Also catches ghost UUIDs: if data.length !== memberIds.length, a UUID didn't exist at all
  if (data.length !== memberIds.length)
    throw new AppError('MEMBER_NOT_FOUND', 404, 'One or more member IDs do not exist.');
}

**Postgres error code mapping (in every service write):**
```js
function mapSupabaseError(err, context = {}) {
  if (err.code === '23505') {                          // unique_violation
    if (err.message.includes('expense_participants'))
      throw new AppError('DUPLICATE_PARTICIPANT', 409, 'A member cannot be added twice to the same expense.');
    throw new AppError('DUPLICATE_ENTRY', 409, err.message);
  }
  if (err.code === '23503')                            // foreign_key_violation
    throw new AppError('REFERENCE_NOT_FOUND', 404, err.message);
  throw new AppError('DB_INSERT_FAILED', 500, err.message);
}
// Usage: if (error) mapSupabaseError(error);
```
> [!NOTE]
> This is what makes the `DUPLICATE_PARTICIPANT` error code in the registry actually reachable. Without this, Postgres `23505` surfaces as generic `DB_INSERT_FAILED`.

**Expense creation — service-level sequencing (no RPC):**

```js
// services/expensesService.js
async function createExpense(data) {
  // 1. Calculate shares based on split_type
  const participants = calculateShares(data);

  // 2. Insert expense row
  const { data: expense, error: expErr } = await supabase
    .from('expenses')
    .insert({ ...expenseFields })
    .select()
    .single();
  if (expErr) throw new AppError('DB_INSERT_FAILED', 500, expErr.message);

  // 3. Insert participant rows (bulk)
  const rows = participants.map(p => ({ expense_id: expense.id, ...p }));
  const { error: partErr } = await supabase
    .from('expense_participants')
    .insert(rows);

  // 4. Compensating rollback if participants insert fails
  if (partErr) {
    await supabase.from('expenses').delete().eq('id', expense.id);
    throw new AppError('DB_INSERT_FAILED', 500, partErr.message);
  }

  return expense;
}
```

> [!WARNING]
> **Transaction Limitation — Known Trade-off**: This approach is **not truly atomic**. In the rare scenario where the expense row is inserted successfully but the participants insert fails *and* the compensating rollback `DELETE` also fails (e.g. due to a network interruption between the two calls), you could end up with an orphaned expense row containing no participants.
>
> This is an acceptable trade-off for this project because:
> - It's easy to understand and debug
> - All business logic stays in one language (JavaScript)
> - The failure window is extremely small in practice
>
> **Future upgrade path**: Wrap both inserts in a Supabase PostgreSQL stored procedure (RPC) called via `supabase.rpc('create_expense_with_participants', {...})`. This executes inside a real `BEGIN / COMMIT / ROLLBACK` transaction, making it truly atomic. This can be dropped in as a single service-layer change without touching controllers or routes.

Same pattern for expense updates: delete existing participants → insert new ones.

